acf663","definition":"Emotional pressure suffered by a human being or other animal.","example":"Go easy on him, he's been under a lot of stress lately.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["accent","emphasis","emphasis"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"uncountable","wordFamily":["antistress","anti-stress","anxiostressive","backstress","combat stress reaction","complex post-traumatic stress disorder","critical incident stress management","cryostress","eigenstress","electrostress","engineering stress","eu-stress","eustress","flow stress","genostress","genotoxic stress","geostress","hyperstress","hypostress","infostress","internal stress","interstress","isostress","macrostress","microstress","mitostress","nitrosative stress","nonstress","nonstress test","osmostress"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stress#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:34267616fe6e6c0c6b","definition":"A suprasegmental feature of a language having additional attention raised to a sound, word or word group by means of of loudness, duration or pitch; phonological prominence.","example":"lexical stress","pos":"noun","synonyms":["emphasis","accent"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"broadly","register":"broadly, countable","wordFamily":["antistress","anti-stress","anxiostressive","backstress","combat stress reaction","complex post-traumatic stress disorder","critical incident stress management","cryostress","eigenstress","electrostress","engineering stress","eu-stress","eustress","flow stress","genostress","genotoxic stress","geostress","hyperstress","hypostress","infostress","internal stress","interstress","isostress","macrostress","microstress","mitostress","nitrosative stress","nonstress","nonstress test","osmostress"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stress#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:ec62d94ec82c5d9fcc","definition":"The suprasegmental feature of a language having additional attention raised to a sound by means of loudness and/or duration; phonological prominence phonetically achieved by means of dynamics as distinct from pitch.","example":"The shift from pitch to stress appears to happen before the other obliques begin merging in the Proto-Italic, Proto-Germanic, Primitive Irish, and Middle Indo-Aryan. But further investigation into the timeline of sound changes […] shows that, at least in Germanic, the oblique and core noun stems sound quite unpredictably different in all these families by the time of the crucial accent shift from pitch to stress. […] once a language becomes stress-sensitive, there seems to be a strong tendency in early Indo-European languages to shift the stress to the first syllable. This change happens shortly after the change to stress accent in Proto-Germanic, Proto-Italic, and Proto-Celtic, and even Thessalian, with evidence from Dybo's Law and Verner's Law left behind to show that sound changes happened after the changes to stress accent.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["stress accent"],"antonyms":["pitch","pitch accent"],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"countable","wordFamily":["antistress","anti-stress","anxiostressive","backstress","combat stress reaction","complex post-traumatic stress disorder","critical incident stress management","cryostress","eigenstress","electrostress","engineering stress","eu-stress","eustress","flow stress","genostress","genotoxic stress","geostress","hyperstress","hypostress","infostress","internal stress","interstress","isostress","macrostress","microstress","mitostress","nitrosative stress","nonstress","nonstress test","osmostress"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stress#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:5c3b5bb205e77069c1"